109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (As we reflect on the 2025 calendar year, it is clear that this period marked a defining moment for the Indigenous Peoples Alliance Ghana \(IPA-GH\). We set out not only to implement projects, but to contribute meaningfully to reshaping climate justice discourse and practice in Ghana. Through the disciplined execution of our strategic pillars, IPA-GH transitioned from an emerging idea into a structured, data-driven movement engaging policymakers, academic institutions, and international development partners.)
/E (As we reflect on the 2025 calendar year, it is clear that this period marked a defining moment for the Indigenous Peoples Alliance Ghana \(IPA-GH\). We set out not only to implement projects, but to contribute meaningfully to reshaping climate justice discourse and practice in Ghana. Through the disciplined execution of our strategic pillars, IPA-GH transitioned from an emerging idea into a structured, data-driven movement engaging policymakers, academic institutions, and international development partners.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The year was characterized by a deliberate shift from localized advocacy to growing national relevance. With a total expenditure of USD 28,520, IPA-GH demonstrated strong fiscal discipline and an ability to deliver high-impact results with limited resources. Operating at the intersection of environmental governance and social equity, we implemented two flagship initiatives: the Marginalized Groups Empowerment for Climate Justice \(MGECJ\) project, supported by the Youth Climate Justice Fund, and the Safeguarding Livelihoods of Rural Communities from Climate-Induced Disasters \(SAFE\) project, funded by the Mastercard Foundation through the Bridge in Agriculture Programme. Together, these interventions advanced our core mission of ensuring that rural women, youth, and indigenous farmers are not only protected from climate risks but are also active contributors to locally grounded solutions.)
/E (The year was characterized by a deliberate shift from localized advocacy to growing national relevance. With a total expenditure of USD 28,520, IPA-GH demonstrated strong fiscal discipline and an ability to deliver high-impact results with limited resources. Operating at the intersection of environmental governance and social equity, we implemented two flagship initiatives: the Marginalized Groups Empowerment for Climate Justice \(MGECJ\) project, supported by the Youth Climate Justice Fund, and the Safeguarding Livelihoods of Rural Communities from Climate-Induced Disasters \(SAFE\) project, funded by the Mastercard Foundation through the Bridge in Agriculture Programme. Together, these interventions advanced our core mission of ensuring that rural women, youth, and indigenous farmers are not only protected from climate risks but are also active contributors to locally grounded solutions.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
