177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The Southern Ghana Workshop served as the foundational effort to disrupt the status quo of exclusion. We meticulously selected 32 participants from various regions in southern Ghana, prioritizing those historically marginalized by systemic inequalities. The cohort comprised 16 youth representatives, identified as the future custodians of climate action, and 16 individuals from indigenous communities, including persons with disabilities, rural farmers, and the elderly. This deliberate mix ensured that the dialogue was not theoretical but grounded in the lived experiences of those most affected. The workshop successfully established a nascent network of advocates equipped to challenge exclusion in their local districts.)
/E (The Southern Ghana Workshop served as the foundational effort to disrupt the status quo of exclusion. We meticulously selected 32 participants from various regions in southern Ghana, prioritizing those historically marginalized by systemic inequalities. The cohort comprised 16 youth representatives, identified as the future custodians of climate action, and 16 individuals from indigenous communities, including persons with disabilities, rural farmers, and the elderly. This deliberate mix ensured that the dialogue was not theoretical but grounded in the lived experiences of those most affected. The workshop successfully established a nascent network of advocates equipped to challenge exclusion in their local districts.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Recognizing the acute vulnerability of the Northern sector to climate-induced desertification and drought, we executed a strategic intervention in this region. This workshop was designed to address the gendered dimensions of climate justice. We convened 42 participants, comprising a decisive majority of 27 women alongside 13 male farmers. This gender composition was intentional, aiming to overturn traditional norms that often silence women in public forums. By positioning women, who are the primary managers of household food security and water resources, as the central knowledge bearers, we achieved a fundamental shift in local power dynamics. These women provided detailed, expert knowledge on drought-resistant crops and water management, validating their role as indispensable architects of climate resilience.)
/E (Recognizing the acute vulnerability of the Northern sector to climate-induced desertification and drought, we executed a strategic intervention in this region. This workshop was designed to address the gendered dimensions of climate justice. We convened 42 participants, comprising a decisive majority of 27 women alongside 13 male farmers. This gender composition was intentional, aiming to overturn traditional norms that often silence women in public forums. By positioning women, who are the primary managers of household food security and water resources, as the central knowledge bearers, we achieved a fundamental shift in local power dynamics. These women provided detailed, expert knowledge on drought-resistant crops and water management, validating their role as indispensable architects of climate resilience.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The "National Research Study" was the intellectual core of the MGECJ project, designed to transition advocacy from anecdote to evidence. We initiated a massive data collection drive, engaging over 300 data contributors from across the nation. To ensure intergenerational equity, 75% of these contributors were youth, with a mandated 15% representation from rural and indigenous communities. The resulting publication, "Assessing the Ghana National Youth Policy and its Impact on Youth Climate Justice in Ghana," provides actionable, data-driven recommendations. It serves as an undeniable mandate for the National Climate Change Policy review board, ensuring that the next iteration of national strategy cannot ignore the specific vulnerabilities and solutions proposed by the next generation.)
/E (The "National Research Study" was the intellectual core of the MGECJ project, designed to transition advocacy from anecdote to evidence. We initiated a massive data collection drive, engaging over 300 data contributors from across the nation. To ensure intergenerational equity, 75% of these contributors were youth, with a mandated 15% representation from rural and indigenous communities. The resulting publication, "Assessing the Ghana National Youth Policy and its Impact on Youth Climate Justice in Ghana," provides actionable, data-driven recommendations. It serves as an undeniable mandate for the National Climate Change Policy review board, ensuring that the next iteration of national strategy cannot ignore the specific vulnerabilities and solutions proposed by the next generation.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (To complement our quantitative data, the "Documenting Everyday Realities" activity utilized the power of visual narrative to humanize complex policy issues. We deployed media teams to three distinct indigenous communities across the Eastern, Bono East, and Northern regions. Through extensive interviews with over 15 individuals, including policymakers, chiefs, and climate-affected farmers, we produced two full-length video documentaries. These assets successfully validated indigenous resilience strategies, packaging them into compelling narratives that resonate with stakeholders who might otherwise remain unmoved by statistical reports alone.)
/E (To complement our quantitative data, the "Documenting Everyday Realities" activity utilized the power of visual narrative to humanize complex policy issues. We deployed media teams to three distinct indigenous communities across the Eastern, Bono East, and Northern regions. Through extensive interviews with over 15 individuals, including policymakers, chiefs, and climate-affected farmers, we produced two full-length video documentaries. These assets successfully validated indigenous resilience strategies, packaging them into compelling narratives that resonate with stakeholders who might otherwise remain unmoved by statistical reports alone.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
