195 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Over the next three years, IPA-GH aims to transition from pilot interventions to systemic change by executing a massive expansion of its advocacy and economic programs. The organization projects growing its internal capacity to 14 full-time co-creators, enabling the training of over 500 marginalized groups in policy advocacy so they can directly lobby for inclusive governance. Simultaneously, the alliance plans to deploy the Ghana Api-Resilience Fund \(GARF\) to launch 25 community-owned beekeeping enterprises, establishing a robust economic foundation that provides climate-resilient incomes for indigenous farmers.)
/E (Over the next three years, IPA-GH aims to transition from pilot interventions to systemic change by executing a massive expansion of its advocacy and economic programs. The organization projects growing its internal capacity to 14 full-time co-creators, enabling the training of over 500 marginalized groups in policy advocacy so they can directly lobby for inclusive governance. Simultaneously, the alliance plans to deploy the Ghana Api-Resilience Fund \(GARF\) to launch 25 community-owned beekeeping enterprises, establishing a robust economic foundation that provides climate-resilient incomes for indigenous farmers.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
196 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (To ensure these initiatives outlast the grant cycle, IPA-GH is implementing a long-term sustainability model designed to operate beyond donor dependency. The projection relies on a circular financial ecosystem where profits from the social enterprises are reinvested into the organization, supplemented by revenue from technical consulting services and indigenous cultural events. This approach guarantees that the organization can continue to fund its operations and expand its impact indefinitely, securing a lasting legacy of self-sufficiency for the communities it serves.)
/E (To ensure these initiatives outlast the grant cycle, IPA-GH is implementing a long-term sustainability model designed to operate beyond donor dependency. The projection relies on a circular financial ecosystem where profits from the social enterprises are reinvested into the organization, supplemented by revenue from technical consulting services and indigenous cultural events. This approach guarantees that the organization can continue to fund its operations and expand its impact indefinitely, securing a lasting legacy of self-sufficiency for the communities it serves.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
